+# FAA October 2003 facility returns and Boston dispatch trail
+
+November 9, 2026 Eastern · B129 / PD07, with PD01–PD04 open. Three public National Archives objects add a ZOB-1 employee-contact certification, a Boston records response and an earlier Indianapolis return. The Indianapolis comparison identifies repeated descriptions of records turned in or reported misplaced. The Boston response identifies a proposed shipment and a soft-copy attachment. These records concern the October 2003 production. The corrected May answer, its receipt and the signed 2006 recommendation outcomes remain open. [National Archives finding aid, Box 97](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid-96-107) · [Source and test pins](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Separate ZOB-1 certification and Boston return
+
+| Source | Recorded scope |
+| --- | --- |
+| **AWA-772**, NAID **34913601**, physical p. 1, OCR lines 9–26 and page face | Dated **October 14, 2003**, from **ZOB-1** to **ADA-2**, referring to an **October 8, 2003** memorandum. The cover certifies contact with employees working **8:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m.** on September 11, 2001, subject to listed exceptions. The object has **18 physical pages**. The first two roster pages, physical pp. 2–3, show contact-date and yes/no fields. The record leaves the underlying items and external delivery to be established. |
+| **AWA-773**, NAID **34913603**, physical p. 1 lines 14–21 and p. 2 lines 1–18 | The **October 15, 2003** memorandum refers to the **October 14** fax request and describes documentation being enclosed. The list says an organizational chart for September 11, 2001 was unavailable. It separately lists staffing dated **September 1, 2001** and **October 15, 2003**, and a chart effective **August 3, 2003**. It lists agreement records and represents that specified agreements were in effect on September 11, 2001. Those are enclosure descriptions and representations about effective dates. |
+| **AWA-773**, physical p. 3, OCR lines 6–27 and page face | A forward dated **October 17, 2003, 09:28 AM** carries a message about sending the requested pages overnight and attaching a soft copy for review or revision. The printed attachment label begins **9-11 Commission Request 10-14-03**. The three-page PDF holds the memorandum and printout. Poppler lists zero embedded files. The native message and attachment, the carrier record and recipient intake remain separate targets. |
+
+Keep the scope of employee contacts in AWA-772 separate from the requested enclosures and dispatch representations in AWA-773. A reported local shipment leaves delivery to headquarters and later provision to the Commission as separate events. [AWA-772 catalogue](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34913601) · [AWA-773 catalogue](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34913603)
+
+## Indianapolis response and roster pagination
+
+- **AWA-782, October 14, 2003**, NAID **34913611**, p. 1 OCR lines 8–18. The **ZID-2** cover says on-duty people were asked about documentation, tapes, personal notes and email, with further contact planned for those unavailable during initial contact. The scan visibly gives **14 OCT 2003**. The OCR output gives **14 OCT 2093**. Both readings are retained at their own source layers. [AWA-782 catalogue](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34913611)
+- **AWA-760, October 30, 2003**, NAID **34913591**, p. 1 page face. The later cover says **all individuals except two** on duty had been asked. Preserve the distinct certification wording. Neither cover identifies every returned item or an item-level delivery receipt. [Held AWA-760 review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/8c169cde263a06e82f18bc69742430f540101402/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-october-tasking-records-2026-11-05/README.md)
+- **Roster comparison.** On a common 150 dpi OCR pass, AWA-782 p. 7 lines 75–82 has **eight** literal **YES, TURNED IN** labels and p. 8 lines 4–16 has **thirteen**. AWA-760 p. 7 has zero matching labels and p. 8 lines 27–67 has **twenty-one**. Both compared sections therefore show **21** occurrences, with different pagination. AWA-782 p. 7 line 74 and AWA-760 p. 8 line 25 each mention a separate personal-notes memo dated **10/14/03**. Both p. 8 rosters also describe copies of traffic-management messages and a Flight 77 progress strip as misplaced during a move (AWA-782 OCR lines 17–19 and AWA-760 lines 68–71). These observations leave the underlying roster edition, exact row correspondence and existence or location of other copies open. The count measures labels in the compared page spans. It supplies no document total.
+
+Both eight-page Indianapolis PDFs were rendered at 96 dpi grayscale for a literal raster hash comparison. No pair matched. The repeated status labels and remark supply content-level anchors across distinct page images. Treating them as independent corroboration of document delivery or loss would require item-level evidence. [Source pins and verification output](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Limits and remaining records
+
+- **PD07.** Obtain the October 8 tasking and October 14 fax request and attachment, the Boston overnight shipment and intake record, native soft copy, itemized Indianapolis returns and the underlying personal-notes memo, traffic-management messages and strip. Verify actual holdings and separate initial responses, internal routing and Commission receipt.
+- **PD01–PD04.** The corrected May 2003 answer, actual delivery, signed consideration or action/no-action, and recommendation-specific response, implementation and closure remain open. Keep the October employee surveys separate from these later records.
+
+Catalogue byte counts matched the three PDFs. The first AWA-772 read stopped at **4,000,001** bytes under a **4,000,000**-byte cap. The exact-size read then returned **4,601,273** bytes with a PDF signature and stored hash. AWA-773 and AWA-782 returned complete **376,757**- and **2,148,312**-byte PDFs. The review OCR-screened AWA-772 pp. 1–3, AWA-773 pp. 1–3 and AWA-782 pp. 1–8. Selected date, scope, enclosure and status regions were checked on renders. The status-count check was limited to pp. 7–8 of the Indianapolis objects. Personnel fields, raw scans and OCR remain in the native source record.
